      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, cutting FF count (and area) versus fully pipelined while still giving 1 result/cycle, so throughput = Fmax and the 32 Msps constraint is met the same way. The trade-off is latency: ceil(N/m)+2 cycles, which is shorter than the fully pipelined N+2, helping the 0.5 us p99 batch bound. I search the same accuracy/width band (W 14-18, N 12-18) and m in 2-4 to map the area-vs-latency trade-off; smaller m gives lower latency at higher FF/area cost, larger m saves area but lengthens the batch. This family is the natural area-reduction lever against pipelined, so I split the budget evenly to cover both and let the Pareto front pick the cheapest feasible point."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "No fleet facts yet, so I start from first principles on the two constraints that actually bind. (1) Throughput >= 32 Msps: both pipelined and pipelined_m emit 1 result/cycle, so this just requires Fmax >= 32 MHz, comfortably achievable in sky130hd for modest widths; it is not the scarce resource. (2) Accuracy max_abs_err <= 2^-12: this is set purely by data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding, and is identical across families. The cheapest designs sit at the low end of W and N that still clear 2^-12, so I centre the search on W 14-18 and N 12-18 with small guards (0-2) and both rounding modes, rather than sweeping the full 8-28 / 4-30 ranges and wasting evals on obviously-too-accurate or too-inaccurate corners. (3) The real differentiator is sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5: a 1 us tick drops 32 requests at once, and all 32 must return within 0.5 us. For a 1-result/cycle unit that is 32 cycles of drain plus pipeline latency; at >=32 MHz that is ~1 us + latency, so the bound is tight and will favour lower latency (smaller N, and pipelined_m over pipelined). I therefore give equal weight to pipelined (baseline, lowest latency for a given N) and pipelined_m (area reduction via m, slightly higher latency) so the merged front spans the area/latency trade-off and the hypervolume captures both the cheapest feasible point and the accuracy headroom. I deliberately keep ranges narrow to concentrate the 100-eval budget on the feasible band instead of the full registry, and I leave the reserved front-mapping to code over these ranges. If the first round shows the batch bound is infeasible even at the lowest latency, I will report infeasible rather than relax the constraint."
